%description:

RFC 9293 — every segment carries the receive window, and the peer sends new data only up to
that edge; when the window is smaller than a full segment, the window and not the segment
size bounds what the sender transmits.

Checks RFC9293-WND-1 and RFC9293-WND-2 (description, with the SHLD-15 rule), and covers
RFC9293-ACKD-1 (must, as a scenario condition) from
doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc9293/catalog.md. The English procedure and the mockup are
in doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks.md#flow-control; the program steps below map one
to one onto its expected observations. Mapping of the mockup: host A = host1, host B =
host2, link L = host1-host2.

checks.md's scenario constants for this check: host B's receive window is 300 octets
(smaller than a 536-octet segment and smaller than any initial congestion window), its
acknowledgment is delayed, and host A's data block is 3000 octets (ten times the window).
Write ISS_A for host A's initial sequence number and W (= 300) for host B's advertised
window: before any data is acknowledged, host A may send octets ISS_A + 1 to ISS_A + W and
no further; after host B acknowledges ISS_A + W + 1 with the window reopened, host A may
continue from ISS_A + W + 1.

Parameters (src/inet/transportlayer/tcp/Tcp.ned), set on host B only: advertisedWindow = 300
(default 14 * mss, i.e. 7504 -- a plain byte count, no unit annotation) and
delayedAcksEnabled = true (default false). The delay itself is not separately configurable:
it is the fixed DELAYED_ACK_TIMEOUT constant in
src/inet/transportlayer/tcp/flavours/TcpBaseAlg.cc, 0.2 s -- exactly checks.md's constant
(permitted, since RFC9293-ACKD-1 only requires less than 0.5 s), so nothing further is set
for the delay itself.

Deviation on tClose: the common mockup's close time (0.4 s) is dropped. Draining the
3000-octet block through a 300-octet window with a 0.2 s delayed ack takes about ten rounds
(~2 s), so 0.4 s would land the close command while the exchange this check examines is
still in progress; the check's own procedure has no close step at all, so tClose = -1s
(never close).

Deviation on observations 3, 4 and 6: as in Rfc9293DataTransfer.test, the data-length
comparison needs ipv4.totalLengthField and ipv4.headerLength, not dissectable at host1.tcp's
own outbound signal, so these observe at host1.eth[0].mac instead. Observations 1, 2 and 5
need no ipv4 field and stay at host1.tcp.

Deviation on host2's app: TcpSinkApp, not TcpEchoApp (the other tests in this suite's own
receiver). TcpEchoApp writes back whatever it receives as soon as it receives it, so its
reply segment always has fresh data to piggyback an immediate acknowledgment on -- it never
lets the delayed-ACK timer fire at all, defeating this check's whole premise (confirmed
empirically: with TcpEchoApp, host A's second 300-octet segment left within
0.00003s of the first, well inside observation 4's 0.15s "never" window, because host B's
echoed reply carried ack = ISS_A + 301 immediately). TcpSinkApp only ever receives, so host
B has nothing of its own to send and must fall back to the delayed, standalone ACK checks.md
requires.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "PacketField.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// The data length of a TCP segment (see Rfc9293DataTransfer.test for the same helper and
// the reasoning behind it).
static double tcpSegmentDataLength(const Packet *packet)
{
    return evalPacketField(packet, "ipv4.totalLengthField").doubleValueInUnit("B")
         - evalPacketField(packet, "ipv4.headerLength").doubleValueInUnit("B")
         - evalPacketField(packet, "tcp.headerLength").doubleValueInUnit("B");
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc9293_flow_control")
        // Observation 1: host A sends its SYN. Record ISS_A. Confirms the connection
        // stimulus.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
                  .capture("issA", "tcp.sequenceNo")
                  .describe("host A's SYN, carrying ISS_A").within(0.5))
        // Observation 2: host A receives host B's SYN-ACK with a window field of 300
        // (RFC9293-WND-1). Record it as W.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .packet("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == true && tcp.ackNo == {issA} + 1 && tcp.window == 300")
                  .capture("w", "tcp.window")
                  .describe("host B's SYN-ACK advertising a 300-octet window").within(0.5))
        // Observation 3: after the send time, host A sends a segment with sequence number
        // ISS_A + 1 that carries exactly W (300) octets of data: the window, not the
        // segment size, bounded it (RFC9293-WND-2). No notBefore is needed: the
        // handshake's own final ACK shares the same starting sequence number but carries
        // no data, so the length check alone already disambiguates the two (see
        // Rfc9293DataTransfer.test's observation 2 for why a notBefore(0.19) here -- this
        // step's anchor is observation 2's SYN-ACK receipt, itself only ~0.1s in -- would
        // wrongly exclude the real 0.2s send time; confirmed empirically).
        .once(on("host1.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.sequenceNo == {issA} + 1")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return tcpSegmentDataLength(c.event.packet) == 300; })
                  .describe("host A's first data segment, bounded to the 300-octet window, not the 536-octet segment size")
                  .within(1.0))
        // Observation 4: for 0.15 s after that segment, host A sends no segment that
        // carries new data beyond ISS_A + W (RFC9293-WND-2). The acknowledgment that would
        // open the window is still 0.05 s away (the 0.2 s delayed-ack timer started when
        // observation 3's segment arrived at host B).
        .never(on("host1.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.sequenceNo > {issA} + 300")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return tcpSegmentDataLength(c.event.packet) > 0; })
                  .describe("any new data beyond the window edge, ISS_A + 300")
                  .within(0.15))
        // Observation 5: host A receives an acknowledgment of ISS_A + W + 1 with a window
        // field of at least W: the receiver accepted the octets and reopened the window.
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetReceivedFromLower")
                  .packet("tcp.ackNo == {issA} + 301 && tcp.window >= 300")
                  .describe("host B's delayed acknowledgment of ISS_A + 301, reopening the window").within(0.5))
        // Observation 6: host A sends a segment with sequence number ISS_A + W + 1 that
        // carries data: the sender resumed exactly at the edge.
        .once(on("host1.eth[0].mac").signal("packetSentToLower")
                  .packet("tcp.sequenceNo == {issA} + 301")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return tcpSegmentDataLength(c.event.packet) > 0; })
                  .describe("host A resuming exactly at the window edge, ISS_A + 301")
                  .within(0.5));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc9293_flow_control compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The mockup of doc/project/evidence/protocol/tcp/checks.md#common-mockup:
// host A -- link L -- host B.
network Rfc9293TcpNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        host1: StandardHost { @display("p=300,100"); }
        host2: StandardHost { @display("p=500,100"); }
    connections:
        host1.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> host2.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = Rfc9293TcpNet
sim-time-limit = 2s
seed-set = 0

# Static ARP keeps the wire free of ARP request/reply noise (also needed since several
# observations read ipv4 fields at the MAC layer).
**.ipv4.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# Every node computes real TCP checksums (checks.md's common mockup constant: the checksum
# is never optional, MUST-2).
**.checksumMode = "computed"

# checks.md#flow-control's scenario constants: host B's receive window is smaller than a
# full segment, and its acknowledgment is delayed.
*.host2.tcp.advertisedWindow = 300
*.host2.tcp.delayedAcksEnabled = true

# The scenario constants of checks.md#common-mockup, except tClose (see the %description
# deviation note: this check's own procedure has no close step, and the windowed transfer
# is still in progress well past the common mockup's 0.4 s close time).
*.host1.numApps = 1
*.host1.app[0].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[0].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[0].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[0].tOpen = 0.1s
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.2s
*.host1.app[0].sendBytes = 3000B
*.host1.app[0].tClose = -1s
*.host2.numApps = 1
*.host2.app[0].typename = "TcpSinkApp"
*.host2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc9293_flow_control: PASS
